#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Generate 07_manuscript/draft/SUPPORTING_TEXTS_DRAFT_2026-10-04.md: a plain-language summary, a journal AI-disclosure paragraph, data/code availability, a cover-letter
skeleton and a declarations checklist. Every number comes from current_figures.compute() and build_manuscript_pieces (verifier-checked); anything the repository cannot know is a
[bracketed placeholder]. Nothing here has been reviewed by a human author, and the headline findings will change if the researcher's open decisions change the corpus.
Run from the project root: python3 code/analysis/build_supporting_texts.py"""
import sys
from pathlib import Path
sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
import current_figures as cf  # noqa: E402
import build_manuscript_pieces as bmp  # noqa: E402

ROOT = cf.ROOT
OUT = ROOT / '07_manuscript/draft/SUPPORTING_TEXTS_DRAFT_2026-10-04.md'


def build():
    F = cf.compute()
    n = F['extraction_rows']; fam = F['effect_size_by_family']
    causal = F['causal_capable_designs']
    n_all, n_non, n_mat = bmp.reextraction_counts()
    L = ["# Supporting texts — DRAFT (2026-10-04)", "",
         "> Generated by `code/analysis/build_supporting_texts.py` from the verifier-checked figures (`00_admin/CURRENT_FIGURES.md`). **Not reviewed by a human author; not for submission.** Square brackets mark what only the authors can supply. The findings may change if the researcher's open decisions (`00_admin/RESEARCHER_DECISION_BRIEF_2026-10-04.md`) change the corpus.", "",
         "## 1. Plain-language summary (about 200 words)", "",
         f"Many households live next to water pipes or sanitation services yet cannot use them, because of rules and paperwork: who counts as eligible, what documents or land title an application needs, what it costs, how much discretion officials have, and whether the rules are enforced. We call this the *legal last mile*. We searched the research literature and, with the help of an AI system working under a human researcher's direction, assessed what studies show about it. We included {n:,} studies from many countries, most published since 2010. Most describe situations and processes; only {causal} used designs that could show that a rule *causes* a change in access. For {F['effect_size_rows']} studies we could compare the direction of results, but the studies measured different things and we did not combine them into one number. Where results could be compared, being recognised or eligible usually went with better access, and help from administrators usually went with better access, while results for barriers and ownership pointed in different directions. Our confidence in any causal claim is low. The AI did most of the work and humans have checked only part of it, so these findings should be treated as a map of the evidence rather than as firm conclusions. [Authors: confirm and simplify.]", "",
         "## 2. AI-use disclosure for the journal (draft paragraph)", "",
         bmp.build().split("## 1. Methods — AI-assisted conduct and disclosure", 1)[1].split("## 2.", 1)[0].strip().split("\n\n")[0],
         "", f"The AI re-read {n_all} included studies from full texts after the first extraction; in {n_mat} of the {n_non} rows that a sparse-record audit had flagged, the first extraction was materially wrong or incomplete (an AI check of AI work, not independent verification). [Journal-specific wording, model version and dates of use: authors to add; confirm the journal's policy on AI-conducted review work and on listing AI as a tool rather than an author.]", "",
         "## 3. Data and code availability (draft)", "",
         "All search records, screening decisions, extraction databases, appraisal forms, synthesis files, generated figures and code are in the project repository [add DOI/URL and archived release once tagged, `CITATION.cff`]. Every number in the paper is re-derived by `python3 code/analysis/verify_repository.py`, which fails if a quoted figure or a generated file is stale. Full texts are not redistributed because of publisher copyright; record identifiers and the retrieval log allow them to be re-obtained. [Authors: confirm licence choices.]", "",
         "## 4. Cover letter skeleton", "",
         "[Journal editor name]\n\nWe submit \"[title]\" for consideration as a [systematic review] in [journal]. The review maps the empirical evidence on how legal and administrative conditions shape whether physical water and sanitation infrastructure translates into household access. "
         f"It identified {bmp.RAW:,} records, assessed {F['full_text_decided']:,} full texts and included {n:,} studies, synthesised without meta-analysis because exposures and outcomes were not comparable ({F['effect_size_rows']} effect-size rows in three families: A {fam['A']}, B {fam['B']}, C {fam['C']}). "
         "The review was conducted with a large language model under the first author's direction; the paper discloses the division of labour, the partial human verification and the main limitations (incomplete full-text retrieval, AI-conducted extraction and appraisal, no pooled estimate). "
         "[Protocol registration status: state precisely, and label any registration as retrospective.] [Suggested reviewers; conflicts; funding: authors.]\n\n[Authors]", "",
         "## 5. Declarations checklist (to complete)", "",
         "| Item | Status |", "|---|---|",
         "| Funding (PRISMA 25) and competing interests (PRISMA 26) | placeholders in `00_admin/disclosures/FUNDING_AND_COMPETING_INTERESTS_TEMPLATE.md` |",
         "| Registration | OSF draft exists, not submitted (decision 9 in the brief) |",
         "| Ethics | no human participants; [confirm with the institution] |",
         "| Author contributions (CRediT) | [authors]; AI system listed as a tool, not an author [confirm journal policy] |",
         "| Citations | none invented; `[ref]` markers in the manuscript draft |",
         "| Second extraction / second screening | not complete (human tasks 1-2 in the brief); the paper must say so |", ""]
    return "\n".join(L) + "\n"


if __name__ == '__main__':
    OUT.write_text(build(), encoding='utf-8')
    print('wrote', OUT.relative_to(ROOT))
